<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><!-- Original-type: ra--><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">10970691</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2374671</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6691371</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1054/bjoc.2000.1371</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Regular Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title><italic>MDR</italic> 1 activation is the predominant resistance mechanism selected by vinblastine in MES-SA cells</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>G K</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Dur&#x000e1;n</surname><given-names>G E</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Mangili</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Beketic-Oreskovic</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Sikic</surname><given-names>B I</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="aff1"><label>1</label>Oncology Division, Department of Medicine, and the Cancer Biology Program, Stanford University School of Medicine, Stanford, CA 94305&#x02013;5151, USA</aff><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>04</day><month>09</month><year>2000</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>10</month><year>2000</year></pub-date><volume>83</volume><issue>7</issue><fpage>892</fpage><lpage>898</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>04</day><month>08</month><year>1999</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>31</day><month>05</month><year>2000</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>04</day><month>06</month><year>2000</year></date></history><copyright-statement>Copyright 2000, Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2000</copyright-year><permissions><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>Single-step selection with vinblastine was performed in populations of the human sarcoma cell line MES-SA, to assess cellular mechanisms of resistance to the drug and mutation rates via fluctuation analysis. At a stringent selection with 20 nM vinblastine, resulting in 5&#x02013;6 logs of cell killing, the mutation rate was 7 &#x000d7; 10<sup>&#x02013;7</sup>per cell generation. Analysis of variance supported the hypothesis of spontaneous mutations conferring vinblastine resistance, rather than induction of adaptive response elements. Surviving clones displayed a stable multidrug resistance phenotype over a 3-month period. All propagated clones demonstrated high levels of resistance to vinblastine and paclitaxel, and lower cross-resistance to doxorubicin and etoposide. Activation of <italic>MDR</italic> 1 gene expression and P-glycoprotein function was demonstrable in all clones. No elevation was found in the expression of the <italic>mrp</italic> gene, the LRP-56 major vault protein and &#x003b2;-tubulin isotypes (M40, &#x003b2;4, 5&#x003b2;, and &#x003b2;9) in these mutants. We conclude that initial-step resistant mechanism in these vinblastine-selected mutants commonly arises from a stochastic mutation event with activation of the <italic>MDR</italic> 1 gene. &#x000a9; 2000 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>multidrug resistance</kwd><kwd>P-glycoprotein</kwd><kwd>vinblastine</kwd><kwd>fluctuation analysis</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>